Copy files from GitHub to Local Folders

You can copy files from GitHub to your local system using two primary methods, 
depending on whether you want to use version control (Git) or just want a one-time copy of the files.

Method 1: Download as a ZIP File (Easiest / No Git Required)
Use this option if you just want a quick, one-time snapshot of the files and do not need to track changes or update them later.
1. Go to the main page of the repository on GitHub.
2. Click the green Code button located near the top right of the file list.
3. Click Download ZIP.
4. Locate the downloaded file on your computer and extract the ZIP archive to access the files.

Method 2: Clone the Repository (Recommended for Developers)
Use this option if you have Git installed and want to copy the entire project along with its history, allowing you to pull future updates or make changes.
1. On the GitHub repository page, click the green Code button.
2. Under the HTTPS tab, click the Copy icon to copy the repository URL.
3. Open your terminal (Mac/Linux) or Git Bash (Windows).
4. Navigate to the folder where you want to save the project using the cd command:bash cd path/to/your/folder
5. Type git clone, paste the URL you copied, and press Enter:bash git clone https://github.com


Method 3: Download a Single Specific File
If you do not want the whole repository and only need a single file:
1. Click on the file inside the GitHub repository to open it.
2. Click the Raw button in the top right corner of the file view.
3. Right-click anywhere on the raw text page and choose Save As... (or press Ctrl + S / Cmd + S) to save it to your system.
